= for combinational logic, &lt;= for clocked logic, every time. Do not mix the two in one block.

5.15 Structural design: module instances
Behavioral modeling describes what a block does. Structural modeling describes a block by how it is composed of simpler modules. Each copy of a smaller module is an instance, and it has a unique name. Here is the smallest useful example, a 4:1 multiplexer assembled from three copies of a 2:1 multiplexer:
module mux2to1 (
    input  logic a, b, sel,
    output logic y
);
  assign y = sel ? b : a;
endmodule

module mux4to1_struct (
    input  logic [3:0] d,
    input  logic [1:0] sel,
    output logic       y
);
  logic y0, y1;                 // wires between the instances

  mux2to1 mux_low   (.a(d[0]), .b(d[1]), .sel(sel[0]), .y(y0));
  mux2to1 mux_high  (.a(d[2]), .b(d[3]), .sel(sel[0]), .y(y1));
  mux2to1 mux_final (.a(y0),   .b(y1),   .sel(sel[1]), .y(y));
endmodule
An instantiation has three parts. First comes the module name (mux2to1), then the instance name you choose (mux_final), then in parentheses the port connections. The notation .a(y0) reads “the module’s port a is connected to my signal y0”. These connections by name make the code easy to read and safe to reorder. There is also a positional form, mux2to1 mux_final (y0, y1, sel[1], y);, which is shorter and fails silently if someone later reorders the ports, so we avoid it. Figure 5.16 shows the result: the tool leaves the three instances as boxes, exactly as the hierarchy appears in the code.






Figure 5.16: The structurally built mux4to1_struct: two instances select within a pair, and a third selects between the two results.




5.15.1 Parameters
A parameter makes a module configurable, which is how one description serves every width. We have been using this all along: flopenr declares parameter WIDTH = 8, and each instance picks its own. The override goes between the module name and the instance name, after a #:
flopenr #(32) pcreg (...);          // a 32-bit instance, by position
flopenr #(.WIDTH(32)) pcreg (...);  // the same, by name
The by-name form is safer when a module has several parameters. A localparam is a constant inside a module that, unlike a parameter, cannot be overridden from outside, which makes it the right choice for derived constants.


5.15.2 A real example: the program counter of HIPV
HIPV’s fetch unit is almost entirely structural. Its program-counter loop is built from three of the leaf cells we wrote above. The code is the actual instantiation, reduced to this loop:
module pc_unit (
    input  logic        clk, reset,
    input  logic        PCEnF,
    input  logic        PCSrcE,
    input  logic [31:0] PCTargetE,
    output logic [31:0] PCF
);
  logic [31:0] PCNextF, PCPlus4F;

  // next PC selection: sequential or redirect from Execute
  mux2 #(32) pcmux (
      .D0(PCPlus4F), .D1(PCTargetE), .S(PCSrcE), .Y(PCNextF));

  // PC register
  flopenr #(32) pcreg (
      .clk(clk), .reset(reset), .en(PCEnF), .D(PCNextF), .Q(PCF));

  // sequential PC
  adder #(32) pcadd4 (
      .A(PCF), .B(32'd4), .Y(PCPlus4F));
endmodule
Everything inside the parentheses is a wire name, and nothing here describes any behavior: it only says which cell goes where. The register holds the PC, the adder computes PC + 4, and the multiplexer chooses between that and a branch target (Figure 5.17). Note how the signals form a loop through the register, which is the only place where the loop is broken by a clock.






Figure 5.17: The PC loop of HIPV, drawn by Yosys with the three cells expanded into their hardware: the $sdffe register, the +4 adder, and the next-PC multiplexer.

5.16 Testbenches
A testbench is a module that tests another module, the device under test (DUT). It applies inputs to the DUT and, ideally, checks that the outputs are right. Its inputs and the expected outputs are the test vectors. A testbench has no ports of its own: it sits at the top of the simulation, so everything it needs is declared inside as plain logic signals and wired to the DUT by name, like any other instance.
module tb_mux4to1;
  logic [3:0] d;
  logic [1:0] sel;
  logic       y;

  mux4to1_struct dut (.d(d), .sel(sel), .y(y));

  initial begin
    d = 4'b1010;
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) begin
      sel = i[1:0];
      #10;
      $display("d=%b sel=%b y=%b", d, sel, y);
      if (y !== d[sel]) $error("sel=%b: expected %b, got %b", sel, d[sel], y);
    end
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
Everything happens in the initial block, which starts at time zero and runs once. It holds d fixed and sweeps sel through all four values. After each change it waits #10 time units, $display prints the inputs and the output, and the if checks y against the bit of d that sel selects. The #10 delay is not needed by the circuit, since a combinational output settles almost instantly. It gives each test case its own slice of simulation time, so the printed lines and the waveform stay readable. The comparison uses !==, which also catches an X on the output where != would stay silent. $finish ends the simulation.
These statements (initial, #10, $display, $finish) exist only in simulation. A synthesizer has no hardware for “wait 10 time units”, which is the line between the code you simulate and the code you synthesize.

5.17 Testbench for a sequential design
A registered design only changes on a clock edge, so the testbench has to supply that clock; nothing else in the simulation will. Here it is for the counter of this chapter:
module tb_counter;
  logic       clk = 0;
  logic       reset = 1;
  logic [3:0] count;

  counter4 dut (.clk(clk), .reset(reset), .count(count));

  always #5 clk = ~clk;              // clock generator: period of 10 units

  initial begin
    #12 reset = 0;                   // release reset between clock edges
    #100;
    $display("count=%0d", count);
    $finish;
  end
endmodule
The separate always #5 clk = ~clk; has no sensitivity list and no guard, so it keeps re-executing for the whole simulation and flips clk every 5 time units. That gives a free-running clock with a period of 10. It runs concurrently with the initial block below it: every initial and always block in a module starts at time zero and proceeds independently, which is why the clock generator lives in its own block.
The stimulus holds reset high for the first clock edge (at time 5), then releases it at time 12, between edges, so that no input changes exactly when the clock rises, the same convention as in our waveforms. After the remaining ten rising edges the counter holds 10, which $display prints. The key difference from the combinational testbench is the waiting. A combinational output is valid almost immediately after its inputs change, but a register updates only once per clock edge, so a sequential testbench must always budget enough simulated time, or wait for a specific number of @(posedge clk) events, before checking the outputs.
$finish matters even more here. The clock generator never terminates on its own, so without an explicit $finish the simulation would run forever.

6.3 Practical case: a traffic light
We now apply the theory to a complete, if small, system: the controller of a traffic light. It is a Moore machine with a data register, it needs time intervals that are long compared with the clock period, and it is built from two modules, a counter that marks time and an FSM that uses it.

6.3.1 Specification
The traffic light cycles through four states, and each state lights a different combination of lamps for a different time:



State
Lights
Time




RED
red
5 s


RED_YELLOW
red and yellow
1 s


GREEN
green
4 s


YELLOW
yellow
2 s



The system clock is 100 MHz, and a synchronous reset puts the light into RED. The outputs Red, Yellow and Green drive the three lamps, and depend only on the state, so the controller is a Moore machine.


6.3.2 System architecture






Figure 6.5: The traffic-light system: the tick generator turns the 100 MHz clock into a one-cycle Tick every millisecond, and the FSM counts those ticks and drives the lamps. Both run on the same clock.



The times are long compared with the clock period. At 100 MHz, 5 s is 500 million cycles, and counting them directly would need a 29-bit counter compared against a large constant in every state. We split the job in two, the same way a UART uses a baud-rate generator (Figure 6.5):

tick_gen is a counter that produces a one-cycle pulse, Tick, once every millisecond.
traffic_fsm is the FSM. It counts those pulses in a data register, MsCount, and when the count reaches the time of the current state, it moves on to the next state.
traffic_light is the top level, which only connects the two.

The FSM still runs on the 100 MHz clock: Tick is an enable, not a slower clock. Every flip-flop in the design sees the same clock edge, which is the synchronous-design rule of the HIPV coding standard, and the time unit of the FSM is now a millisecond, so the times in the specification appear in the code unchanged.


6.3.3 The 1 ms tick generator
The tick generator is a counter with a comparator. It counts from 0 to TICK_CYCLES - 1, raises Tick for the one cycle in which it holds that last value, and starts again from 0:
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// tick_gen
//
// Purpose: One-cycle Tick pulse every TICK_CYCLES clock cycles
//          (1 ms with the default value and a 100 MHz clock).
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

module tick_gen #(parameter TICK_CYCLES = 100_000) (
  input  logic clk, reset,
  output logic Tick);

  localparam CNT_WIDTH = $clog2(TICK_CYCLES);

  logic [CNT_WIDTH-1:0] Count;

  always_ff @(posedge clk)
    if (reset || Tick) Count &lt;= '0;
    else               Count &lt;= Count + 1;

  assign Tick = (Count == TICK_CYCLES - 1);

endmodule
The period of Tick is exactly TICK_CYCLES clock cycles, because the counter spends one cycle at each of the values 0 to TICK_CYCLES - 1. With the default of 100 000 and a 100 MHz clock, that is 1 ms. The counter width is derived from the parameter with $clog2, which the tool evaluates at compile time, and it is a localparam because nobody outside should change it. Tick itself is a Moore-style output of the counter: it depends only on the register Count, so it is clean for a whole clock cycle.


6.3.4 The ASM chart of the traffic light






Figure 6.6: ASM chart of the traffic-light FSM. Each column is one state: the state box with its Moore outputs, the decisions on Tick and MsCount, and the conditional updates of MsCount.



The ASM chart (Figure 6.6) has one column per state. The state boxes hold the Moore outputs, the lamps that are on. There are no Mealy outputs, so the conditional boxes contain only the updates of the data register MsCount.
Read the RED column. In this state the red lamp is on. If Tick is 0, we take the 0 exit back to the entry of RED: nothing changes. If Tick is 1, a millisecond has passed, and the second decision checks whether it was the last one. If not, MsCount is incremented and we stay in RED. If it was, MsCount is cleared and the FSM moves to RED_YELLOW. The other three columns are the same, each with its own time and its own successor, and YELLOW returns to RED.
The - 1 in the decisions comes from counting from 0. After RED_MS - 1 ticks, the count holds RED_MS - 1, and the tick that arrives while the count holds that value is the RED_MS-th one. The FSM leaves the state on exactly that tick.


6.3.5 The FSM in SystemVerilog
The code follows the chart box by box, and uses the FSMD pattern for MsCount:
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// traffic_fsm
//
// Purpose: Traffic-light controller. Cycles through RED, RED_YELLOW,
//          GREEN and YELLOW, and holds each state for its own number
//          of 1 ms ticks.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

module traffic_fsm #(parameter RED_MS = 5000, RED_YELLOW_MS = 1000,
                               GREEN_MS = 4000, YELLOW_MS = 2000) (
  input  logic clk, reset,
  input  logic Tick,
  output logic Red, Yellow, Green);

  localparam CNT_WIDTH = 16;               // enough for 65 s per state

  typedef enum logic [1:0] {RED, RED_YELLOW, GREEN, YELLOW} state_t;

  state_t               State, NextState;
  logic [CNT_WIDTH-1:0] MsCount, MsCountNext;

  // state register
  always_ff @(posedge clk)
    if (reset) begin
      State   &lt;= RED;
      MsCount &lt;= '0;
    end else begin
      State   &lt;= NextState;
      MsCount &lt;= MsCountNext;
    end

  // next-state logic
  always_comb begin
    NextState   = State;                   // default: stay in the state
    MsCountNext = MsCount;                 //          and keep the count
    if (Tick) MsCountNext = MsCount + 1;   // count the elapsed milliseconds
    case (State)
      RED:
        if (Tick && MsCount == RED_MS - 1) begin
          NextState   = RED_YELLOW;
          MsCountNext = '0;
        end
      RED_YELLOW:
        if (Tick && MsCount == RED_YELLOW_MS - 1) begin
          NextState   = GREEN;
          MsCountNext = '0;
        end
      GREEN:
        if (Tick && MsCount == GREEN_MS - 1) begin
          NextState   = YELLOW;
          MsCountNext = '0;
        end
      YELLOW:
        if (Tick && MsCount == YELLOW_MS - 1) begin
          NextState   = RED;
          MsCountNext = '0;
        end
      default: NextState = RED;
    endcase
  end

  // output logic: the lights depend on the state only
  assign Red    = (State == RED) || (State == RED_YELLOW);
  assign Yellow = (State == RED_YELLOW) || (State == YELLOW);
  assign Green  = (State == GREEN);

endmodule
The state register stores the control state and the data register MsCount, and loads both together. On reset the FSM goes to RED with the count at 0, which is the reset arrow of the chart.
The next-state logic starts with the defaults: stay in the current state and keep the count. They cover the Tick = 0 exits of every column without a single else. The next line is the MsCount++ box, which is the same in every state, so we write it once: on every tick, count. The case then handles the last tick of each state. It overrides both defaults: it selects the next state and clears the counter. Because this assignment comes later in the block, it wins over the increment, as the blocking = guarantees. The default branch is not reachable with four states in two bits, but it keeps the machine safe if the encoding changes.
The output logic is three assign statements, one per lamp, each a function of State alone: this is a Moore machine, so the lamps change only at a clock edge, when the state changes. The light times are parameters, so another junction, with other times, is a different instance of the same module.


6.3.6 The traffic light in the waveform






Figure 6.7: traffic_fsm in simulation with the times scaled down: one tick every 2 clock cycles, and states of 4, 1, 3 and 2 ticks. RY is RED_YELLOW.



With the real parameters, one traffic-light cycle lasts 12 s, or 1.2 billion clock cycles, far too many to simulate or to draw. Because every time is a parameter, the testbench can instantiate both modules with small values instead: TICK_CYCLES = 2, and states of 4, 1, 3 and 2 ticks (Figure 6.7). The design is the same; only the numbers are smaller.
reset is high for the first edge, so in column 0 the FSM is in RED and MsCount is 0. Tick is high in every second column. At each edge that follows a tick, MsCount goes up by one: it is 1 in columns 2 and 3, 2 in columns 4 and 5, and 3 in columns 6 and 7. In column 7 Tick is high and MsCount is 3, which is RED_MS - 1, so the edge starting column 8 moves the FSM to RED_YELLOW and clears the count. RED lasted 8 cycles, exactly 4 ticks. RED_YELLOW lasts only one tick: already in column 9, Tick is high while MsCount is 0, which is RED_YELLOW_MS - 1, so at column 10 the FSM moves to GREEN. MsCount stays 0 across that change, because it is cleared, not incremented. GREEN counts 0, 1, 2 and YELLOW 0, 1, and at column 20 the FSM is back in RED.
The bottom three rows are the lamps. They change only at the edges where State changes, and the red lamp stays on through RED_YELLOW, as the output logic says.


6.3.7 Putting it together
The top level is purely structural, as the HIPV convention asks for a module that only connects blocks. It is Figure 6.5 in code: one instance of each module, wired by name.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////
// traffic_light
//
// Purpose: Traffic light: a 1 ms tick generator driving the
//          traffic-light FSM.
///////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////////

module traffic_light(
  input  logic clk, reset,
  output logic Red, Yellow, Green);

  logic Tick;

  tick_gen #(.TICK_CYCLES(100_000)) msgen (
    .clk(clk), .reset(reset), .Tick(Tick));

  traffic_fsm lightfsm (
    .clk(clk), .reset(reset), .Tick(Tick),
    .Red(Red), .Yellow(Yellow), .Green(Green));

endmodule
The split is what makes the design easy to change. A board with a 50 MHz clock needs a different TICK_CYCLES, and a different junction needs different light times; each is one parameter in one instance, and neither touches the logic of the FSM. The same pattern, a counter that produces a periodic tick and an FSM that uses the tick as an enable, appears in every serial interface: in a UART, the tick marks the duration of one bit, and the FSM steps through the start bit, the data bits and the stop bit.

7.3 System level
At the system level, a design is a set of blocks and the connections between them. The HIPV SoC (rtl/top/hipv_soc.sv) consists of:

the RISC-V core, hipv_core, with two OBI master ports, one for instructions and one for data;
an OBI crossbar that connects the two masters to five slaves;
a 64 KiB RAM that holds both the program and the data;
three peripherals: a UART, a timer and a GPIO block.

Nothing at this level says how any block works. The decisions made here are about the system as a whole: which blocks there are, what bus connects them and with which protocol, where each block sits in the address map, and how fast the clock runs. For HIPV, the bus is OBI, the address map gives every slave its own window, and the clock is 50 MHz on the Nexys A7-100T board.

7.6 Gate level
At the gate level, the design is a netlist: a list of cells and of the wires that connect them. It is produced from the RTL by synthesis, and nobody writes it by hand. What the cells are depends on the target. For an ASIC, they are the gates and flip-flops of a standard-cell library. For HIPV on the Xilinx xc7a100t FPGA, they are the primitives of the 7-series fabric that we met in the FPGA chapter:

every register bit of flopenr becomes an FDRE, a flip-flop with a clock enable and a synchronous reset, which is exactly what flopenr describes;
the multiplexers, decoders and the ALU logic become LUT6 look-up tables;
the adders use the CARRY4 fast-carry chains of the slices;
the 64 KiB RAM becomes 16 RAMB36E1 block RAMs.

The schematics drawn by Yosys in the chapter on designing with SystemVerilog are small examples of this level. The netlist is also the level at which timing is checked: only when we know which cells and wires a path goes through can the tools tell whether it fits in the 20 ns clock period of 50 MHz.

8.2 Introduction
The instruction fetch unit is the first stage of the HIPV pipeline (see the overview in the previous chapter). Before the core can execute an instruction, it must fetch it from memory, and which instruction it fetches is determined by the program counter (PC), the register that holds the address of the current instruction. RISC-V instructions are 32 bits wide, four bytes, so without jumps the program counter advances by four after every instruction.
To do this job, the IFU needs five things:

two registers, one that holds the PC and one that holds the fetched instruction;
an adder that computes the address of the next instruction, PC + 4;
a multiplexer that chooses the next PC: the sequential PC + 4, or the target of a jump or taken branch;
a port to the instruction memory, through which it sends the address and receives the instruction;
a controller that decides when each register is loaded, when a request goes to memory, and when the fetched instruction is ready for the next stage.

The first four form the datapath, and they are built from the generic blocks of the chapter on designing with SystemVerilog. The controller is a finite state machine, and it is where most of the thinking in this chapter goes.

8.4 Structure of the IFU






Figure 8.3: The IFU: the PC loop (pcmux, pcreg, pcadd4), the instruction register (instrreg) and the controller (ifuctrl).



Figure 8.3 shows the IFU. It has three parts.
The PC loop updates the program counter. The multiplexer pcmux chooses the next PC, PCNextF: the sequential PCPlus4F when PCSrcE is 0, and the jump target PCTargetE when it is 1. The register pcreg holds the current PC, PCF. It has an enable, PCEnF, driven by the controller, so the PC changes only when the controller allows it. The adder pcadd4 computes PCF + 4. The current PC is also the address of the memory read, so ObiAddrF is simply PCF.
The instruction register instrreg stores the fetched instruction. Its input is the memory data ObiRdataF, and its enable, InstrEnF, is also driven by the controller: the register must capture ObiRdataF in exactly the cycle in which ObiRvalidF says that it is valid.
The controller ifuctrl manages the flow through the unit: it watches the memory, Decode and Execute, and drives the two enables, the memory request and ValidF. It is the subject of Section 8.5.

8.4.1 The building blocks
The datapath uses three generic modules from rtl/generic, which we met in the chapter on designing with SystemVerilog. All three are parameterized by WIDTH, and the IFU uses them with 32 bits.
flopenr is a register with a synchronous reset and an enable. At a rising edge it clears to zero if reset is high, otherwise it loads D if en is high, and otherwise it keeps its value. The missing else branch is what makes it hold. The reset value zero is also the reset vector of HIPV: after reset, the first instruction is fetched from address 0.
module flopenr #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
  input  logic             clk, reset, en,
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] D,
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0] Q);

  always_ff @(posedge clk)
    if (reset) Q &lt;= '0;
    else if (en) Q &lt;= D;

endmodule
mux2 is a two-input multiplexer: Y is D1 when S is 1 and D0 when it is 0. It is purely combinational, one continuous assignment with the conditional operator.
module mux2 #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] D0, D1,
  input  logic             S,
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0] Y);

  assign Y = S ? D1 : D0;

endmodule
adder adds two numbers. The result has the same width as the operands, so the carry out is dropped. For the PC this is the intended behavior: the address wraps around from 0xFFFFFFFC to 0.
module adder #(parameter WIDTH = 8) (
  input  logic [WIDTH-1:0] A, B,
  output logic [WIDTH-1:0] Y);

  assign Y = A + B;

endmodule


8.4.2 The IFU in SystemVerilog
The body of ifu is purely structural: it is Figure 8.3 written as code, one instance per block, with the ports connected by name. The only internal signals are the next PC and the two enables.
  logic [31:0] PCNextF;
  logic        PCEnF, InstrEnF;

  // fetch control FSM
  ifuctrl ctrl(.clk(clk), .reset(reset), .ObiGntF(ObiGntF), .ObiRvalidF(ObiRvalidF),
               .ReadyD(ReadyD), .PCSrcE(PCSrcE), .ObiReqF(ObiReqF), .PCEnF(PCEnF),
               .InstrEnF(InstrEnF), .ValidF(ValidF));

  // next PC selection: sequential or redirect from Execute
  mux2 #(32)    pcmux(.D0(PCPlus4F), .D1(PCTargetE), .S(PCSrcE), .Y(PCNextF));

  // PC register
  flopenr #(32) pcreg(.clk(clk), .reset(reset), .en(PCEnF), .D(PCNextF), .Q(PCF));

  // sequential PC
  adder #(32)   pcadd4(.A(PCF), .B(32'd4), .Y(PCPlus4F));

  // fetched instruction register
  flopenr #(32) instrreg(.clk(clk), .reset(reset), .en(InstrEnF), .D(ObiRdataF), .Q(InstrF));

  // the OBI address is the current PC
  assign ObiAddrF = PCF;
Note that the select of pcmux is PCSrcE directly, not a signal of the controller. The controller only decides when the PC changes, through PCEnF, and the multiplexer decides to what.

8.5 The IFU controller
The controller ifuctrl manages the flow of instructions through the IFU. It has three tasks:

generate the internal enables InstrEnF and PCEnF, which decide when the instruction register and the PC register are loaded;
issue the memory requests through the OBI port, with ObiReqF;
tell Decode that the IFU has finished fetching an instruction, with ValidF.

Why do we need a controller at all, when the datapath looks so simple? For three reasons. First, fetching an instruction does not happen in one cycle. The memory needs time to grant the request and more time to respond, and the controller must watch ObiGntF and ObiRvalidF to know where the transaction is. Second, a jump can arrive at any time. The IFU must then restart fetching from the new address and, if an instruction from the old address is still on its way from memory, throw it away when it arrives. Third, Decode may not be ready to accept the instruction, and then the IFU must freeze, with its outputs stable, until it is.
All three depend on what has happened before: whether a request has been granted, whether an instruction has arrived, whether it has been handed over. The controller must remember this, so it is a finite state machine.

8.5.1 The states






Figure 8.4: The IFU controller as a state diagram, with the transitions only.



A fetch goes through three steps, and each becomes a state:

REQUEST: the IFU initiates the fetch. It sends a request to the memory and waits for the memory to accept it.
WAIT_RESP: the request has been accepted, and the IFU waits for the response, the instruction.
DONE: the instruction has arrived and sits in the instruction register. The IFU offers it to Decode and waits until Decode takes it.

In SystemVerilog, the states are an enumerated type, encoded in two bits. Following the FSM pattern of the previous chapter, the controller has a current-state and a next-state signal. It also has a second current/next pair, DiscardF and NextDiscardF, which we explain next.
typedef enum logic [1:0] {REQUEST, WAIT_RESP, DONE} statetype;

statetype State, NextState;
logic     DiscardF, NextDiscardF;
Figure 8.4 shows the states and the transitions between them. The following sections derive the transitions one state at a time.


8.5.2 The discard flag
Besides the state, the controller keeps one more bit, DiscardF, a pending request to discard the instruction that is on its way. Consider a jump that arrives after the memory has granted a request, but before it has responded. The response will come, because a granted transaction cannot be cancelled, and it will carry the instruction from the old address, which is now on the wrong path. The IFU must not hand that instruction to Decode, and it must not start the fetch from the new address before the stale response has arrived. Otherwise it would take the stale response as the answer to the new request, since responses carry no tag.
The difficulty is time. PCSrcE is high for a single cycle, but on a slow memory the stale response may arrive many cycles later, when PCSrcE is long gone. So the controller needs one bit of memory that says “the response I am waiting for belongs to the wrong path”: DiscardF. It is set when PCSrcE arrives while a transaction is outstanding, which is in WAIT_RESP, or in REQUEST in the very cycle the grant arrives. It is cleared when the awaited response arrives, and that response is dropped instead of captured.


8.5.3 Transitions






Figure 8.5: ASM chart of the IFU controller: the transitions and the updates of DiscardF. Where no box is shown, DiscardF keeps its value.



Figure 8.5 shows the transitions as an ASM chart, one column per state, together with the updates of DiscardF in the rounded conditional boxes. Let us go through it state by state.
From REQUEST. First, we ask whether the memory has accepted our request, by checking ObiGntF. If not, we stay in REQUEST and keep asking. If it has, the transaction is committed and we move to WAIT_RESP. Before we go, we check whether a jump has occurred in this same cycle (PCSrcE). If so, the response that will come belongs to the old PC, and we raise DiscardF to 1. A jump in REQUEST without the grant needs no flag: nothing has been committed yet, the PC is loaded with the target in this cycle, and the request simply continues with the new address.
From WAIT_RESP. First, we ask whether the instruction has arrived from memory, by checking ObiRvalidF. If not, we keep waiting in WAIT_RESP. While we wait, a jump may occur, so we check PCSrcE and, if it is 1, raise DiscardF: the response we are waiting for is now stale. If the instruction has arrived, we check whether we must discard it, by reading DiscardF and PCSrcE. DiscardF covers a jump in an earlier cycle, and PCSrcE a jump in this very cycle. If either is 1, we drop the instruction, clear the pending discard, and restart the fetch by moving to REQUEST. Otherwise, the instruction is good, and we proceed to DONE.
From DONE. We have the instruction, and now we must finish with it. There are two ways out. In the normal one, ReadyD is 1: Decode takes the instruction, and we can fetch the next one. In the other, a jump has occurred, the instruction we hold is on the wrong path, and we must restart the fetch from the target. If either condition is met, we move to REQUEST; otherwise we stay in DONE. No transaction is outstanding in DONE, so DiscardF does not change here.
The flag is cleared only in WAIT_RESP, because that is the only state in which a response can arrive. On the path to DONE, the flag is already 0, so no box is needed there.


8.5.4 The controller in SystemVerilog
The code follows the chart column by column. State and DiscardF are updated in the same register block, with a synchronous reset to REQUEST and no pending discard:
  // state register
  always_ff @(posedge clk)
    if (reset) begin
      State    &lt;= REQUEST;
      DiscardF &lt;= 1'b0;
    end else begin
      State    &lt;= NextState;
      DiscardF &lt;= NextDiscardF;
    end
The next-state logic computes both next values in one always_comb block. It starts with the defaults, hold the state and hold the flag, and each branch of the case overrides only what changes:
  // next state logic
  always_comb begin
    NextState    = State;  // defaults: hold
    NextDiscardF = DiscardF;
    case (State)
      // the grant commits the transaction on this edge, so a redirect
      // in the same cycle already has a wrong-path response coming
      REQUEST:   begin NextState    = ObiGntF ? WAIT_RESP : REQUEST;
                       NextDiscardF = ObiGntF & PCSrcE; end
      // the awaited response clears the flag; with no response yet, a
      // redirect makes the outstanding one stale
      WAIT_RESP: if (ObiRvalidF) begin NextState    = (DiscardF | PCSrcE) ? REQUEST : DONE;
                                       NextDiscardF = 1'b0; end
                 else NextDiscardF = DiscardF | PCSrcE;
      DONE:      if (PCSrcE | ReadyD) NextState = REQUEST;
      default:   NextState = REQUEST;
    endcase
  end
Keeping DiscardF in the same current/next pattern as State matters in WAIT_RESP. When the response arrives, the decision (DiscardF | PCSrcE) reads the current DiscardF, while the clear goes to NextDiscardF and only takes effect at the next edge. The flag is therefore still valid in the cycle in which the stale response is examined. In REQUEST the flag is always 0, so NextDiscardF = ObiGntF & PCSrcE is the same as “set it on a jump in the cycle of the grant”.






Figure 8.6: The NextDiscardF logic drawn as hardware. The two priority multiplexers clear the flag on a response and set it on a jump during an outstanding transaction; otherwise the register holds its value. Since a response arrives only in WAIT_RESP, this is the same function as the case in the code.



Figure 8.6 shows what the DiscardF lines become in hardware: one flip-flop and two multiplexers. The first sets the flag when PCSrcE arrives while a transaction is outstanding; the second, which has priority, clears it when the response arrives. If neither applies, the flip-flop gets its own output back, and holds.


8.5.5 The outputs
The four outputs are combinational functions of the state, the flag and the inputs:
  // outputs
  assign ObiReqF  = (State == REQUEST);
  assign PCEnF    = PCSrcE | ((State == DONE) & ReadyD);
  assign InstrEnF = (State == WAIT_RESP) & ObiRvalidF & ~DiscardF & ~PCSrcE;
  assign ValidF   = (State == DONE) & ~PCSrcE;

ObiReqF is high in REQUEST. It depends only on the state, so it is a Moore output. The request stays high until the grant moves the FSM to WAIT_RESP, exactly as the OBI address phase requires.
PCEnF lets the PC change in two cases. On a jump, the PC loads the target immediately, in any state, because pcmux selects PCTargetE whenever PCSrcE is 1. In DONE, when Decode takes the instruction (ReadyD), the PC advances to PCPlus4F. The PC must not advance earlier, because PCF is a stage output that belongs to the instruction that Decode has not taken yet.
InstrEnF loads the instruction register in the cycle the response arrives (WAIT_RESP and ObiRvalidF), unless the response is stale (DiscardF) or a jump arrives in the same cycle (PCSrcE). Both are exactly the conditions under which the FSM returns to REQUEST instead of going to DONE.
ValidF is high in DONE, while the instruction waits for Decode. A jump turns it off at once, in the same cycle, so that Decode never takes an instruction from the wrong path.

All outputs except ObiReqF also depend on the inputs, so they are Mealy outputs: they react in the same cycle, without waiting for a state change. That is what lets the IFU take a jump in the very cycle PCSrcE is high.


8.5.6 The controller in the waveform






Figure 8.7: Two fetches. Each passes through REQUEST, WAIT_RESP and DONE. InstrEnF follows ObiRvalidF, and PCEnF rises only when Decode takes the instruction: in cycle 6 for the first one, after two cycles of ReadyD = 0.



Figure 8.7 shows the controller at work, on a memory that grants after one wait cycle and responds one cycle after the grant. In cycle 2 the grant arrives, and the FSM moves to WAIT_RESP. In cycle 3 the response arrives; InstrEnF is high, so at the end of the cycle the instruction register captures I0, and the FSM moves to DONE. From cycle 4, ValidF offers I0 to Decode, but Decode is busy for two cycles. In cycle 6 ReadyD rises, PCEnF follows it, and at the end of the cycle the PC advances to 0x4 and the FSM starts the next fetch.
Each instruction spends at least one cycle in each state, so the IFU fetches at most one instruction every three cycles, even with an ideal memory that grants at once. This is a deliberate simplification that keeps the controller small. A faster IFU would issue the next request before the previous response arrives, and keep the extra instructions in a small buffer.






Figure 8.8: A redirect while a transaction is outstanding. The jump in cycle 2 sets DiscardF and loads the PC with the target T. The response for the old PC A arrives in cycle 4 and is dropped; the fetch restarts from T in cycle 5.



Figure 8.8 shows the discard flag at work, on a memory that takes three cycles to respond. In cycle 1 the request for address A is granted. In cycle 2, while the IFU waits for the response, Execute resolves a jump to T: PCSrcE is high, so PCEnF loads T into the PC, and NextDiscardF is 1. In cycle 4 the response for A arrives. DiscardF is 1, so InstrEnF stays low, the instruction register keeps its old value, and the FSM returns to REQUEST while the flag clears. From cycle 5 on, the IFU fetches from T as usual, and in cycle 9 it offers I(T) to Decode.
Without the flag, the FSM in cycle 4 would capture I(A) and offer it to Decode together with PCF = T: the wrong instruction, with the address of another one.
